 # LOD Selection and Culling
 
-Also called: Level of Detail Selection, Hierarchical Culling, Multi-resolution Approximation
+Also called: Level-of-Detail Selection, LOD Culling
 
 ## Overview
-LOD Selection and Culling is a family of techniques that optimize volume rendering performance by dynamically selecting data resolutions based on spatial hierarchy, viewer proximity, or visual importance, while discarding invisible regions [S1][S2, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods like [Mesh Simplification](1032-mesh-simplification.md) by operating on volumetric data structures (bricks, octrees, splats) rather than polygonal meshes, and from [Wavelet-based Volume Rendering](1052-wavelet-based-volume-rendering.md) by focusing on runtime selection and culling logic rather than the specific compression transform itself.
+LOD Selection and Culling refers to the family of techniques that dynamically determine the appropriate spatial resolution for rendering subregions of a volume dataset based on viewer proximity, screen footprint, or data variation, while discarding invisible regions to reduce computational load. It differs from sibling methods like [Mesh Simplification](1032-mesh-simplification.md) by operating on volumetric data structures (bricks, octrees, splats) rather than polygonal meshes, and from [Wavelet-based Volume Rendering](1052-wavelet-based-volume-rendering.md) by focusing on the selection logic and culling of pre-existing or generated multi-resolution blocks rather than the decomposition algorithm itself.
 
