 ### [Streaming and Level-of-Detail (LOD) selection](3087-streaming-and-level-of-detail-lod-selection.md)
-Streaming and Level-of-Detail (LOD) selection is a method for visualizing massive scenes that exceed rendering or memory budgets by dynamically selecting data resolution based on viewpoint position and screen footprint. It solves the problem of navigating arbitrarily large datasets without stalling the frame rate or exceeding memory limits by combining additive ladders (progressive detail) with substitutive levels (coarse approximations) and spatial culling. It sets itself apart from its siblings by explicitly managing the temporal aspect of data arrival and resolution selection through a "level-of-detail grammar" that prioritizes visual coverage preservation during streaming, whereas methods like [Hierarchical Culling & Multi-resolution Approximation](2010-hierarchical-culling-multi-resolution-approximation.md) focus on static hierarchy traversal, and [Focus Point Based Brick LOD Selection](5282-focus-point-based-brick-lod-selection.md) relies on fixed spatial bricks rather than splat-based footprints. See [Streaming and Level-of-Detail (LOD) selection](3087-streaming-and-level-of-detail-lod-selection.md).
