 ### [Multi-resolution LOD Volume Rendering with Temporal Coherence](5717-multi-resolution-lod-volume-rendering-with-temporal-coherenc.md)
-Multi-resolution LOD Volume Rendering with Temporal Coherence is a technique that assigns different resolution levels to subvolumes based on their visual importance (such as projection area, gaze distance, or viewer proximity) to optimize rendering performance while maintaining visual quality. It distinguishes itself from standard LOD methods by explicitly addressing the visual instability caused by frequent resolution changes during view updates, using a temporal coherence mechanism to prevent flickering. Unlike [Hierarchical Culling & Multi-resolution Approximation](2010-hierarchical-culling-multi-resolution-approximation.md) which may rely on strict hierarchical traversal, this method allows arbitrary texture resolutions for subvolumes provided level transitions are constrained. See [Multi-resolution LOD Volume Rendering with Temporal Coherence](5717-multi-resolution-lod-volume-rendering-with-temporal-coherenc.md).
