 ### [Flat blocking multi-resolution volume rendering](6428-flat-blocking-multi-resolution-volume-rendering.md)
-Flat blocking multi-resolution volume rendering is a strategy for loading and rendering large volume datasets by dividing the volume into sub-blocks of equal physical size but assigning each block a different resolution (Level of Detail, LOD) based on its internal data variation. It solves the problem of memory bandwidth and rendering time for large volumes by avoiding uniform high-resolution storage. Unlike hierarchical structures that change spatial extent, this method keeps block dimensions constant while varying the voxel count within them. It is distinguished from its siblings by its explicit use of a "flat" blocking structure where LOD is determined per block via a variation heuristic, often implemented using min/max octrees. See [Flat blocking multi-resolution volume rendering](6428-flat-blocking-multi-resolution-volume-rendering.md).
