 ## Background
-The concept of Level of Detail (LOD) in volume rendering has evolved from simple distance-based culling to complex, data-driven hierarchical structures. Early approaches focused on reducing the number of voxels or cells rendered based on their distance from the camera [S1]. As datasets grew larger, methods like [Streaming and Level-of-Detail (LOD) selection](3087-streaming-and-level-of-detail-lod-selection.md) and [Hierarchical Culling & Multi-resolution Approximation](2010-hierarchical-culling-multi-resolution-approximation.md) introduced spatial partitioning (bricks, octrees) to manage memory and bandwidth more efficiently. The introduction of temporal coherence techniques [S1] addressed the visual artifacts caused by rapid LOD changes during camera movement. More recent developments, such as [Flat blocking multi-resolution volume rendering](6428-flat-blocking-multi-resolution-volume-rendering.md) and [Focus Point Based Brick LOD Selection](5282-focus-point-based-brick-lod-selection.md), emphasize data-awareness and user interaction, allowing for non-uniform resolution assignment based on local data variance or user focus [S1][S2, unofficial]. The rise of web-based rendering has also spurred methods like [Pyramid Level Model Selection](5338-lod-selection-and-culling.md) to handle heterogeneous data structures and network constraints [S2, unofficial].
+The concept of Level of Detail (LOD) in volume rendering originated from the need to handle large datasets interactively. Early approaches focused on hierarchical data structures like octrees and bricks to enable efficient culling and resolution selection. The integration of LOD with streaming architectures became prominent with the rise of out-of-core rendering techniques, allowing datasets larger than main memory to be visualized. Recent developments emphasize GPU-based dynamic calculation and temporal coherence to maintain visual stability during camera movement.
 
